<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Lübeck</h1><p class="st-meta">1180 CE – 1937 CE (approx.) · capital Eutin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The only prince-bishopric to survive the Reformation in Protestant hands, ruled from Eutin by Holstein-Gottorp princes until secularised in 1803.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> moved the bishopric of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">Oldenburg</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-holstein">Holstein</a> to the new town of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-lubeck">Lübeck</a> in 1160, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> gathered estates in eastern Holstein. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry's</a> fall in 1180 they held these lands as imperial princes. Conflicts with the citizens of the Free and Hanseatic City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> led them to govern from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eutin">Eutin</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The territory was a small rural principality of lakes and estates around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eutin">Eutin</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> the cathedral chapter turned Lutheran and from 1586 elected princes of the house of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-holstein-gottorp">Holstein-Gottorp</a>, an arrangement the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> protected. One of them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-frederick-king-of-sweden">Adolf Frederick</a>, became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1751. Eutin grew into a small court town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, later called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> of the North.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 the see was secularised as the Principality of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-lubeck">Lübeck</a> and joined to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-oldenburg">Duchy of Oldenburg</a>, whose ruler had been its last prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. It stayed Oldenburg's exclave until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greater-hamburg-act">Greater Hamburg Act</a> of 1937 merged it into Prussian <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/provisional-government-of-schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-lubeck.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Lübeck" width="320" height="288"><figcaption>Image: Roke (map), Hanno Sandvik (addition of colours) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HRR_1648_Hst_L%C3%BCbeck.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1180 CE – 1937 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Eutin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adolf Frederick, Peter Friedrich Ludwig of Oldenburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, then Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Lübeck, Principality of Lübeck</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1558&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-lubeck" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Lübeck on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4915 321 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Lübeck lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5235 577L5229 694M5235 577L5183 604M5235 577L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5183 604h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5235 577h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5235 577h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Eutin. <a href="/atlas?year=1558&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-lubeck">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
